+++ b/crates/rightkit-process/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,6 +2,14 @@
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+Added (Unix implemented, Windows stubs answer `Unsupported`; no new dependency, no feature):
+
+- **Identity-checked adopt and terminate.** `ProcessIdentity { pid, started: StartTime, executable }` with `of_pid`, `of_current`, `of_child(&OwnedChild)` (read under the child's identity proof and lock), `check()`, and a stable one-line text form (`Display`/`FromStr`; the crate has no serde, so there is no `Serialize`). `AdoptedProcess::adopt_checked(&ProcessIdentity)` answers `AdoptError::{Gone, Replaced { found }, ExecutableMismatch { expected, found }, Unverifiable { kind, reason }}`; "cannot describe" is never a match, and pids 0 and 1 are never adopted. `AdoptedProcess::stop_tree(StopOptions { grace, kill_wait })` stops the process group the adopted process leads (the process alone when it leads none), proves the identity again before every signal, waits on the kernel's exit events (no timer), survives a leader that exits mid-stop, and returns a `StopReport { scope, term_sent, kill_sent, outcome }`. `AdoptedProcess::new` still proves nothing and cannot be stopped (`NotVerified`); the compile-fail test that it has no `terminate_tree` still holds. `AdoptedProcess` stays `Copy`. The start time is `proc_pidinfo` on macOS and `/proc/<pid>/stat` plus the boot id on Linux; the one reader is `identity::describe_state` (`describe` is unchanged and now calls it), the comparison compares the start time and not the parent, because a stored identity outlives its parent.
+- **`MachineLock`**: a machine-wide single-holder lock with a holder record. `MachineLock::acquire(name, dir, MachineLockOptions)` takes an OS advisory lock (`std::fs::File::lock`, released by the kernel when the holder dies) and writes `<name>.holder.json` atomically (holder identity, since, a string `details` map, `idle`/`busy`, and `waiters` assembled from the live registration files). The lock is the truth: a record of a dead holder is ignored and reported as `took_over()`. A busy lock is waited on in the lock call itself (helper thread, reused per file, no retry loop) and reports `MachineLockError::Busy` carrying the holder record on timeout. A holder hears a waiter arrive without polling (`waiter_events()` / `on_waiter(callback)`) through a FIFO doorbell it owns. `machine_dir(app)` gives `/tmp/<app>-<uid>` created `0700` and validated (real directory, owned by this user), and `acquire` refuses a link, another user's directory, or one writable by group or others. `MachineLock::peek` reads who holds a lock without creating anything. `rightkit-fs` is not used (no cycle, but its lock waits with a retry delay).
+- **`CallerLease`** and **`LeaseClaim`**: a worker-side guard for work that must not outlive its caller. The worker watches the caller's process with the kernel's exit event (kqueue `NOTE_EXIT` on macOS, pidfd on Linux) after proving the caller's identity; a new caller may claim the lease inside `LeaseOptions::grace` by writing its identity (the claim rings the worker's FIFO doorbell; the file is also read when the grace ends); when the grace ends unclaimed `on_lost(LeaseLoss)` runs once. `LeaseClaim::release()` ends the watch with no `on_lost`. A heartbeat file is only the fallback (`LeaseOptions::with_heartbeat_ttl`, `heartbeat_only`), for hosts with no exit watch.
+- Internal: `exit_watch::poll_readable` and `ExitWatch::event_fd` (one `poll(2)` over several events), `owned::wait_out` and `unix_child::group_members_besides` are `pub(crate)`, a minimal JSON reader and writer (`json`) for the two record files, `record_file` (bounded reads, atomic replace), and the `doorbell` and `machine_sys` platform modules whose Windows halves are stubs with the exact Win32 calls to implement in their module documentation.
+- Tests (macOS and Linux): `tests/adopted_identity.rs`, `tests/machine_lock.rs`, `tests/caller_lease.rs` with real processes, plus unit tests in the new modules.
+
 - Fixed (Windows, fourth read finding 6): an ordinary spawn could run while an allowlisted spawn (`inherit_handles_only`) had the caller's handles raised for inheritance, and its child then inherited those private handles. Both spawn paths of this crate now take one lock that covers raising the flags, creating the process and restoring them. Added `with_windows_spawn_lock` so code that creates processes by other means (a plain `std::process::Command::spawn`) can join the same serialisation; a spawn made outside it, in a process that also uses `inherit_handles_only`, is still exposed. A regression test stops an allowlisted spawn while its handle is inheritable and shows an ordinary spawn creates nothing until the handle is private again.
 - Fixed (Windows): `wait_or_kill` terminates the retained job when the leader exits within `grace`, so descendants that outlived the leader no longer survive; the result is still `Exited` with the leader's status.
 - Fixed (macOS): `inherit_handles_only` now lists the executed child's own descriptor table after the fork and seals every unlisted descriptor, instead of trusting a bound taken before the fork. A descriptor opened, or a soft limit raised, between those two moments can no longer leak into the program. A table that cannot be listed fails the spawn.
