@@ -168,6 +172,187 @@ in which that could happen. The rule that replaces it:
 - `tests/parent_death.rs` covers the Linux and macOS behavior by re-executing the test binary as the parent,
   SIGKILLing it, and checking the child pid. The group-wide and outlived-engine cases run on macOS only.
 
+## Identity-checked adoption and termination
+
+An app that remembers a pid across its own restart (a leftover engine it must stop, a caller it
+must watch) remembers a `ProcessIdentity` instead: the pid, the process start time and the
+executable, read while the process was known to be the right one.
+
+```rust,ignore
+use rightkit_process::{AdoptError, AdoptedProcess, OwnedChild, ProcessIdentity, StopOptions};
+
+// At spawn: store the one-line text form with the app's own state.
+let identity = ProcessIdentity::of_child(&child)?;          // proven under the child's lock
+let stored: String = identity.to_string();                  // pid=4242;start=macos:...;exe=%2F...
+
+// After a restart:
+let identity: ProcessIdentity = stored.parse()?;
+match AdoptedProcess::adopt_checked(&identity) {
+    Ok(adopted) => {
+        let report = adopted.stop_tree(StopOptions::default())?;   // TERM, grace, KILL; bounded
+        // report.scope, report.term_sent, report.kill_sent, report.outcome
+    }
+    Err(AdoptError::Gone) => { /* nothing to do */ }
+    Err(AdoptError::Replaced { .. }) => { /* the pid is someone else's now: leave it alone */ }
+    Err(AdoptError::ExecutableMismatch { .. }) => { /* same start time, another program */ }
+    Err(error) => { /* Unverifiable: this host cannot tell. Never treat it as a match. */ }
+}
+```
+
+- **Identity** is `pid` + `StartTime` + optional `executable`. `StartTime` is the platform value the
+  crate already records for its own children: macOS `proc_pidinfo` start time (seconds and
+  microseconds), Linux field 22 of `/proc/<pid>/stat` (ticks since boot) together with the boot id
+  (ticks restart at zero on every reboot), Windows the creation `FILETIME`. The executable is
+  `proc_pidpath` on macOS and the `/proc/<pid>/exe` link on Linux (a `" (deleted)"` suffix from an
+  updated program file is ignored). The text form round-trips, including executable paths that are
+  not UTF-8 (percent-encoded bytes). The crate has no serde dependency, so there is no `Serialize`
+  impl; store the string.
+- **One rule, one reader.** The kernel's description comes from `identity::describe_state` (the
+  reader the crate's own children already use); `process_identity::verify_parts` is the single
+  comparison. Unlike a child's own check it compares the start time and not the parent: a stored
+  identity outlives its parent (that is why it was stored).
+- **`adopt_checked`** answers `Gone`, `Replaced { found }` (alive with another start time),
+  `ExecutableMismatch { expected, found }` or `Unverifiable { kind, reason }` (the host cannot
+  describe processes, cannot read this one, or the identity came from another kind of host;
+  `is_unsupported()` tells the first case). Pids 0 and 1 are never adopted. An exited, unreaped
+  process is adopted (its group may have survivors).
+- **`stop_tree`** stops the process group the adopted process leads (`getpgid(pid) == pid`, the
+  shape `OwnedCommand` gives every child); a process that leads no group is stopped alone and
+  `StopReport::scope` says `Process`. It opens the exit event, proves the identity, proves it again
+  immediately before `SIGTERM`, sleeps in `poll(2)` on the leader's and every group member's exit
+  event for at most `grace`, proves again before `SIGKILL`, and waits at most `kill_wait`. A leader
+  that exits mid-stop does not end the stop: the survivors pin the group id and are still reached.
+  The report says which signals were delivered, to what, and what survived (`Survivors(pids)`).
+  Stopping the current process, or a group this process is in, is refused. The residual is the one
+  in `identity`: the microseconds between a proof and its signal.
+- **Platforms.** macOS and Linux implemented. On Linux a leader-only signal goes through the pidfd,
+  so no pid number is involved. Windows: `of_pid`/`of_child`/`adopt_checked`/`stop_tree` answer
+  `Unsupported` (typed: `AdoptError::Unverifiable` with kind `Unsupported`). The Windows session
+  implements `observe` (`OpenProcess` + `GetProcessTimes` creation time + `QueryFullProcessImageNameW`)
+  and `stop` (handle-pinned identity, Toolhelp descendants, `TerminateProcess`); the exact data is
+  in the doc comments of `src/observe.rs` and `src/adopted.rs`.
+
+## Machine-wide lock
+
+`MachineLock` is the admission gate for a resource that must exist once per machine whichever app
+asks (one model server, one GPU job). It replaces hand-rolled lock-plus-record code.
+
+```rust,ignore
+use rightkit_process::{machine_dir, HolderState, MachineLock, MachineLockError, MachineLockOptions};
+use std::time::Duration;
+
+let dir = machine_dir("my-suite")?;                     // /tmp/my-suite-<uid>, owner-only, validated
+let options = MachineLockOptions::default()
+    .with_timeout(Duration::from_secs(120))
+    .with_detail("model", "qwen21");
+match MachineLock::acquire("sd-server", &dir, options) {
+    Ok(lock) => {
+        if let Some(old) = lock.took_over() { /* a dead holder's record: sweep what it started */ }
+        lock.set_detail("port", "8123")?;
+        let waiters = lock.on_waiter(|arrival| { /* an idle holder drops the lock here */ })?;
+        // ... work; lock.set_state(HolderState::Idle) when it could give the lock up at once
+    }
+    Err(MachineLockError::Busy(busy)) => { /* busy.holder: who is in the way, busy.waited */ }
+    Err(error) => { /* invalid name, directory refused, I/O */ }
+}
+```
+
+- **The lock is the truth; the record is a note.** The lock is `std::fs::File::lock` (`flock` on
+  macOS and Linux, `LockFileEx` on Windows) on `<name>.lock`: the kernel releases it when the
+  holder dies, `SIGKILL` included. `<name>.holder.json` (holder `ProcessIdentity`, `since`, a
+  caller-supplied `details` map of strings, `state` `idle`/`busy`) is written atomically by the
+  holder only. A record whose holder is not a live process (`HolderRecord::holder_status()`) never
+  decides anything: the next holder overwrites it and reports it as `took_over()`. `waiters` is
+  assembled from the registration files when the record is read and is not in the JSON. JSON is
+  encoded by a small internal module (no serde in this crate).
+- **`rightkit-fs` is not used.** `rightkit-fs` has the primitive (`FileLock::acquire_kind` with
+  `LockKind::Flock` is `flock(2)`, the same call as `std::fs::File::lock`), and this crate could
+  depend on it without a cycle (`rightkit-fs` depends on `serde`, `serde_json`, `sha2`,
+  `thiserror` and `libc`, and on no RightKit crate). It is not used because `FileLock` waits by
+  sleeping a 50 ms poll between tries (`rightkit-fs/src/lock.rs`, `wait_or_fail`), which this
+  crate may not do, and because the std lock is stable at this crate's MSRV and keeps `libc` the
+  only dependency. **Give one lock file one kind for every user:** `rightkit-fs` documents that
+  `flock` and `fcntl` locks on one file are not guaranteed to exclude each other (they did on
+  macOS Darwin 27 and need not on Linux), and its default kind is `fcntl`. A lock file that an
+  older build takes through `FileLock` (GenRight's `JobLocks`) must not be shared with
+  `MachineLock`; migrate every user of that name at once, or use a new name.
+- **Waiting is the kernel's.** `acquire` tries the lock; if it is held and a timeout was given, it
+  blocks in the lock call on a helper thread and the caller waits on a condition variable with a
+  deadline. There is no retry loop and no timer. On timeout: `MachineLockError::Busy` carrying the
+  holder record. A helper whose caller timed out stays parked in the kernel until the holder lets go,
+  then releases at once (nobody wants it); later attempts on the same file reuse it, so retrying
+  against a wedged holder does not add a thread per attempt.
+- **Where it lives.** `machine_dir(app)` is `/tmp/<app>-<uid>` on Unix, the literal `/tmp` and not
+  `$TMPDIR`/`$HOME` (QA runners and isolated callers remap those), created `0700` and validated on
+  every use through a descriptor opened without following links: a real directory owned by this
+  user; a wider mode on a directory this user owns is tightened; another user's directory or a link
+  is an error. `acquire` refuses any directory that is a link, belongs to another user, or is
+  writable by group or others.
+- **A holder hears a waiter arrive without polling.** A waiter registers
+  (`<name>.waiter.<pid>.<n>`, one file per waiter so no two processes write the same file) and
+  writes one line to the holder's doorbell. A regular file has no notification that is the same on
+  kqueue and inotify, so the doorbell is a **FIFO** (`<name>.bell`) the holder owns and sleeps on in
+  `poll(2)`: `waiter_events()` returns a `WaiterEvents` (`wait(timeout)`, and `AsRawFd` for a
+  holder with its own loop), `on_waiter(callback)` runs a callback on a thread until its guard is
+  dropped. A FIFO path has no 104-byte limit like a socket path.
+- **Rules.** A process that holds a lock must not acquire it again (it would wait out its timeout).
+  `MachineLock::peek` briefly takes a free lock, so a zero-timeout `acquire` racing it may report
+  `Busy` once. Dropping the lock removes the record and the doorbell, then releases.
+- **Windows** is a stub (`Unsupported`): the lock call and the record code are portable, the
+  Windows session supplies `machine_dir` (`%PROGRAMDATA%\<app>\runtime` with an owner-only DACL),
+  the file helpers and the doorbell (a named event plus a message file); see
+  `src/machine_sys.rs` and `src/doorbell.rs`.
+
+## Caller lease
+
+`CallerLease` is the worker-side guard for work that must not outlive whoever asked for it (a
+render job, a model server), without heartbeats and without polling.
+
+```rust,ignore
+use rightkit_process::{CallerLease, LeaseClaim, LeaseOptions};
+use std::time::Duration;
+
+// Caller:
+let claim = LeaseClaim::claim_current(&lease_path, "app")?;   // writes its identity, rings the worker
+// ... and when it finishes normally and wants the worker to carry on unguarded:
+claim.release()?;
+
+// Worker:
+let lease = CallerLease::watch(
+    &lease_path,
+    None,                                              // or Some(identity) handed over on the command line
+    LeaseOptions::default().with_grace(Duration::from_secs(60)),
+    |loss| { /* cancel the job, stop the server: runs once */ },
+)?;
+```
+
+- **The worker watches the caller's process**, not a file: it proves the caller's identity and
+  sleeps in `poll(2)` on the kernel's exit event (kqueue `NOTE_EXIT` on macOS, a pidfd on Linux,
+  the same `exit_watch` the crate's own children use). The watch is bound to the process, not to
+  its number. The worker learns of the caller's death the moment the kernel reports it.
+- **Re-attach window.** When the caller exits the worker waits `grace` for a new caller to
+  **claim** the lease: `LeaseClaim::claim` writes the new caller's identity into the lease file and
+  rings the worker's doorbell (a FIFO `<lease>.bell`), so the worker re-reads it at once and watches
+  the new caller. A claim is accepted only if it names a live process (a claim naming a dead one
+  does not restart the grace). The ring is a hint; the worker also reads the file when the grace
+  ends, so a claim whose ring was lost still counts if it was written in time. A claim over a caller
+  that is still alive moves the watch to the new caller.
+- **`on_lost` runs once**, on the watcher thread, when the grace ends unclaimed
+  (`LeaseLoss { holder, reason: HolderExited | HolderReplaced | HeartbeatExpired, grace }`). A caller
+  that calls `release()` ends the watch with `LeaseEnd::Released` and no `on_lost`. Dropping the
+  `CallerLease` stops the watch (`Stopped`, no `on_lost`); `detach()` keeps it for the life of the
+  process; `wait()` and `stop()` join and return the `LeaseEnd`. Dropping a `LeaseClaim` does not
+  release the lease: a caller that exits is what the lease is for.
+- **A heartbeat is only the fallback**, for a host that cannot watch the caller's process
+  (`LeaseOptions::with_heartbeat_ttl`) or when forced (`heartbeat_only(ttl)`): the caller calls
+  `LeaseClaim::heartbeat()` more often than the ttl (the crate starts no heartbeat thread) and the
+  worker times out on the ttl in `poll(2)`. A heartbeat rings the doorbell, so one that resumes inside
+  the grace revives the lease. Heartbeats are judged by the wall clock.
+- **Windows**: `CallerLease::watch` answers `Unsupported`; `LeaseClaim` (file only) is portable. The
+  Windows session waits with `WaitForMultipleObjects` on the caller's process handle, a named event
+  for the doorbell, and a stop event, after proving the caller by `GetProcessTimes`; see
+  `src/caller_lease.rs`.
+
