+//! A caller lease: a guard, on the side of the work, for work that must not outlive whoever asked
+//! for it.
+//!
+//! A worker that runs for a long time on behalf of a caller (a render job, a model server) used
+//! to be tied to nothing: when the caller died, the worker and everything it started kept
+//! running, holding memory nobody would ever collect. A lease ties the worker to the asker
+//! without heartbeats and without the worker polling anything:
+//!
+//! * **The lease is a file**, written by the caller ([`LeaseClaim`]): the caller's
+//!   [`ProcessIdentity`], a label, when it claimed and its last heartbeat. Replaced atomically,
+//!   under a small writer lock so a claim, a heartbeat and a release do not interleave. The file
+//!   is the truth about who the caller is.
+//! * **The worker watches the caller's process** ([`CallerLease::watch`]), not the file. It
+//!   proves the caller's identity (the pid still starts at the recorded time, see
+//!   `process_identity`) and sleeps in `poll(2)` on the kernel's exit event for that process: a
+//!   kqueue `EVFILT_PROC`/`NOTE_EXIT` knote on macOS, a pidfd on Linux (the same mechanism as
+//!   `exit_watch`, which the crate's own children use). The watch is bound to the process, not to
+//!   its number, so a recycled pid can neither satisfy it nor block it. The worker learns of the
+//!   caller's death the moment the kernel reports it. Nothing wakes on a timer.
+//! * **A grace window lets a new caller take over.** When the caller exits, the worker does not
+//!   stop at once: a detached worker is meant to survive a caller restart. For
+//!   [`LeaseOptions::grace`] it waits for a new caller to **claim** the lease: write its own
+//!   identity into the lease file ([`LeaseClaim::claim`]), which also rings the worker's doorbell
+//!   (a FIFO beside the file, see `doorbell`) so the worker re-reads it at once. The claim is
+//!   accepted only if it names a live process; the worker then watches that process instead and
+//!   the grace is over. The doorbell is a hint: when the grace ends the worker reads the file
+//!   once more, so a claim whose ring was lost still counts if it was written in time.
+//! * **When the grace ends unclaimed, `on_lost` runs, once**, on the watcher's thread: the app
+//!   cancels its job and stops its server there. A caller that finishes normally calls
+//!   [`LeaseClaim::release`] instead, which removes the lease; the worker then ends without
+//!   calling `on_lost` ([`LeaseEnd::Released`]).
+//! * **A heartbeat is only the fallback** for a host that cannot watch the process (no pidfd and
+//!   no kqueue: [`LeaseOptions::heartbeat_ttl`] set and the watch unavailable), or when forced
+//!   (`LeaseOptions::heartbeat_only`). The caller then calls [`LeaseClaim::heartbeat`] more
+//!   often than the `ttl`; the worker sleeps in `poll(2)` with the ttl as its timeout and treats a
+//!   lease whose heartbeat is older than the ttl as lost. This is the one place a timer decides:
+//!   the caller's cadence is the app's, the crate starts no heartbeat thread. A heartbeat is
+//!   judged by the wall clock, so a clock set backwards by more than the ttl can keep a dead
+//!   caller alive for that long.
+//!
+//! # Rules
+//!
+//! * One watcher per lease path: [`watch`](CallerLease::watch) on a path that another live
+//!   watcher already listens on fails with `AddrInUse`.
+//! * Dropping a [`CallerLease`] stops the watch (no `on_lost`). Keep it for as long as the worker
+//!   runs, or [`detach`](CallerLease::detach) it to watch for the life of the process.
+//! * Dropping a [`LeaseClaim`] does **not** release the lease: a caller that exits is exactly what
+//!   the lease is for. Call [`release`](LeaseClaim::release) to hand the worker back.
+//! * A worker learns its caller's identity from whoever started it: pass
+//!   [`ProcessIdentity`]'s text form on the command line, or read it from the lease file. Do not
+//!   ask the worker for `getppid` after the fact: a parent that already died has been replaced
+//!   by init.
+//! * The lease directory must be private to the user (the file, its writer lock and its doorbell
+//!   are created owner-only and never through links, but the directory is the app's to protect).
+//!
+//! # Platforms
+//!
+//! macOS and Linux are implemented and tested with real processes. On Windows
+//! [`CallerLease::watch`] answers `Unsupported`; [`LeaseClaim`] (file only) is portable. What the
+//! Windows session implements: prove the caller with `OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION
+//! | SYNCHRONIZE)` and compare `GetProcessTimes` creation time with `identity.started`, then wait
+//! with `WaitForMultipleObjects` on the process handle (signalled at exit), a named auto-reset
+//! event standing in for the doorbell (`doorbell` documents the `ring` side) and a stop event;
+//! `GetExitCodeProcess` is not needed, the handle is signalled when the process ends.
+
+use crate::{
+    doorbell::{ring, StopSender},
+    json::{self, Json},
+    machine_sys as sys,
+    process_identity::ProcessIdentity,
+    record_file::{lock_exclusive, read_small, write_atomic},
+};
+use std::{
+    fs, io,
+    path::{Path, PathBuf},
+    thread,
+    time::{Duration, SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH},
+};
+
+const LEASE_VERSION: u64 = 1;
+const YEAR: Duration = Duration::from_secs(365 * 24 * 3600);
+
+/// How a [`CallerLease`] judges its caller.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct LeaseOptions {
+    /// How long after the caller is lost a new caller may still claim the lease. `ZERO` is no
+    /// window: `on_lost` runs as soon as the caller is gone (a claim written in that instant
+    /// still counts). Default 30 seconds.
+    pub grace: Duration,
+    /// Enables the heartbeat fallback: where the host cannot watch the caller's process, the
+    /// caller must refresh [`LeaseClaim::heartbeat`] at least this often. `None` (the default):
+    /// no fallback, and a host that cannot watch refuses the lease (`Unsupported`).
+    pub heartbeat_ttl: Option<Duration>,
+    /// Judge the caller by its heartbeat alone, without watching its process. For hosts where
+    /// watching a foreign process is not permitted, and for tests. Needs `heartbeat_ttl`.
+    pub heartbeat_only: bool,
+}
+
+impl Default for LeaseOptions {
+    fn default() -> Self {
+        Self {
+            grace: Duration::from_secs(30),
+            heartbeat_ttl: None,
+            heartbeat_only: false,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+impl LeaseOptions {
+    pub fn with_grace(mut self, grace: Duration) -> Self {
+        self.grace = grace;
+        self
+    }
+
+    /// Allows the heartbeat fallback with this ttl.
+    pub fn with_heartbeat_ttl(mut self, ttl: Duration) -> Self {
+        self.heartbeat_ttl = Some(ttl);
+        self
+    }
+
+    /// Judges the caller by its heartbeat alone, with this ttl.
+    pub fn heartbeat_only(mut self, ttl: Duration) -> Self {
+        self.heartbeat_ttl = Some(ttl);
+        self.heartbeat_only = true;
+        self
+    }
+
+    fn validate(&self) -> io::Result<()> {
+        let invalid = |why: &str| io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::InvalidInput, why.to_string());
+        if self.grace > YEAR {
+            return Err(invalid("a lease grace over a year is not a grace"));
+        }
+        match self.heartbeat_ttl {
+            Some(ttl) if ttl.is_zero() || ttl > YEAR => {
+                Err(invalid("a heartbeat ttl is between 1 ns and a year"))
+            }
+            None if self.heartbeat_only => Err(invalid("heartbeat_only needs a heartbeat_ttl")),
+            _ => Ok(()),
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// Why a caller was given up on.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum LossReason {
+    /// The caller's process exited.
+    HolderExited,
+    /// The caller's pid names another process now (it exited and the number was reused), or the
+    /// process runs another program than the lease records.
+    HolderReplaced,
+    /// The heartbeat fallback: no heartbeat arrived within the ttl.
+    HeartbeatExpired,
+}
+
+/// What `on_lost` is told.
+#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct LeaseLoss {
+    /// The caller that was lost.
+    pub holder: ProcessIdentity,
+    pub reason: LossReason,
+    /// The grace that passed without a claim.
+    pub grace: Duration,
+}
+
+/// How a watch ended.
+#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub enum LeaseEnd {
+    /// The lease was lost and unclaimed after the grace; `on_lost` ran.
+    Lost(LeaseLoss),
+    /// The caller released the lease; `on_lost` did not run.
+    Released,
+    /// The [`CallerLease`] was stopped or dropped; `on_lost` did not run.
+    Stopped,
+    /// The watch could not continue (the string says why); `on_lost` did not run.
+    Failed(String),
+}
+
+/// What the lease file says.
+#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct LeaseRecord {
+    /// The caller.
+    pub holder: ProcessIdentity,
+    /// Whatever the caller wrote to say what kind of caller it is (`"cli"`, `"app"`).
+    pub label: String,
+    pub claimed: SystemTime,
+    /// The last [`LeaseClaim::heartbeat`] (the claim time until the first one).
+    pub heartbeat: SystemTime,
+}
+
+impl LeaseRecord {
+    /// The lease at `path`; `None` when there is none, or the file cannot be read or is not a
+    /// lease.
+    pub fn read(path: &Path) -> Option<LeaseRecord> {
+        decode(&read_small(path)?)
+    }
+}
+
+fn unix_ms(time: SystemTime) -> u64 {
+    time.duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH)
+        .ok()
+        .and_then(|elapsed| u64::try_from(elapsed.as_millis()).ok())
+        .unwrap_or(0)
+}
+
+fn encode(record: &LeaseRecord) -> String {
+    let mut text = json::write(&Json::Object(vec![
+        ("version".to_string(), Json::Uint(LEASE_VERSION)),
+        ("holder".to_string(), Json::Str(record.holder.to_string())),
+        ("label".to_string(), Json::Str(record.label.clone())),
+        (
+            "claimed_unix_ms".to_string(),
+            Json::Uint(unix_ms(record.claimed)),
+        ),
+        (
+            "heartbeat_unix_ms".to_string(),
+            Json::Uint(unix_ms(record.heartbeat)),
+        ),
+    ]));
+    text.push('\n');
+    text
+}
+
+fn decode(text: &str) -> Option<LeaseRecord> {
+    let value = json::parse(text)?;
+    if value.get("version")?.as_u64()? != LEASE_VERSION {
+        return None;
+    }
+    let time = |key: &str| -> Option<SystemTime> {
+        UNIX_EPOCH.checked_add(Duration::from_millis(value.get(key)?.as_u64()?))
+    };
+    Some(LeaseRecord {
+        holder: value.get("holder")?.as_str()?.parse().ok()?,
+        label: value.get("label")?.as_str()?.to_string(),
+        claimed: time("claimed_unix_ms")?,
+        heartbeat: time("heartbeat_unix_ms")?,
+    })
+}
+
+/// `<path>.<suffix>`: the doorbell and the writer lock live beside the lease file.
+fn sibling(path: &Path, suffix: &str) -> io::Result<PathBuf> {
+    let name = path
+        .file_name()
+        .and_then(|name| name.to_str())
+        .ok_or_else(|| {
+            io::Error::new(
+                io::ErrorKind::InvalidInput,
+                "a lease path names a file with a UTF-8 name",
+            )
+        })?;
+    Ok(path.with_file_name(format!("{name}.{suffix}")))
+}
+
+fn bell_path(path: &Path) -> io::Result<PathBuf> {
+    sibling(path, "bell")
+}
+
+/// Runs `f` while holding the lease's writer lock, so a claim, a heartbeat and a release see each
+/// other whole. The lock is held for the length of one small file operation; the blocking
+/// `lock()` is the kernel's, not a retry.
+fn with_writer_lock<R>(path: &Path, f: impl FnOnce() -> io::Result<R>) -> io::Result<R> {
+    let guard = sys::open_lock_file(&sibling(path, "lck")?, true)?;
+    lock_exclusive(&guard)?;
+    let result = f();
+    let _ = guard.unlock();
+    result
+}
+
+/// Tells the watcher on `path`, if there is one, to re-read the lease. A hint: nobody listening
+/// is not an error, and a lost ring is made good by the read at the end of the grace.
+fn notify(path: &Path, message: &[u8]) {
+    if let Ok(bell) = bell_path(path) {
+        let _ = ring(&bell, message);
+    }
+}
+
+/// The caller's side of a lease: it holds the lease while the caller lives.
+///
+/// The lease belongs to the *process* named in the claim, not to this value. Dropping a
+/// `LeaseClaim` leaves the lease standing; [`release`](Self::release) ends it.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+pub struct LeaseClaim {
+    path: PathBuf,
+    holder: ProcessIdentity,
+}
+
+impl LeaseClaim {
+    /// Writes `holder` as the lease's caller and tells the worker's watcher, if one is
+    /// listening. The last claim wins: a claim over a lease whose caller is still alive moves the
+    /// watch to the new caller. The directory of `path` must exist.
+    pub fn claim(path: &Path, holder: ProcessIdentity, label: &str) -> io::Result<LeaseClaim> {
+        let now = SystemTime::now();
+        let record = LeaseRecord {
+            holder: holder.clone(),
+            label: label.to_string(),
+            claimed: now,
+            heartbeat: now,
+        };
+        with_writer_lock(path, || write_atomic(path, &encode(&record)))?;
+        notify(path, b"claim\n");
+        Ok(LeaseClaim {
+            path: path.to_path_buf(),
+            holder,
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// [`claim`](Self::claim) for the current process.
+    pub fn claim_current(path: &Path, label: &str) -> io::Result<LeaseClaim> {
+        Self::claim(path, ProcessIdentity::of_current()?, label)
+    }
+
+    /// The caller this claim names.
+    pub fn holder(&self) -> &ProcessIdentity {
+        &self.holder
+    }
+
+    /// Refreshes the heartbeat. `Ok(false)` when the lease no longer names this caller (another
+    /// caller claimed it, or it was released). Only needed for the heartbeat fallback.
+    pub fn heartbeat(&self) -> io::Result<bool> {
+        let refreshed = with_writer_lock(&self.path, || {
+            let Some(current) = LeaseRecord::read(&self.path) else {
+                return Ok(false);
+            };
+            if current.holder != self.holder {
+                return Ok(false);
+            }
+            let record = LeaseRecord {
+                heartbeat: SystemTime::now(),
+                ..current
+            };
+            write_atomic(&self.path, &encode(&record))?;
+            Ok(true)
+        });
+        // Outside the writer lock. In the heartbeat fallback the watcher reads the lease when
+        // this arrives, which is what lets a heartbeat that resumes inside the grace revive it.
+        if matches!(refreshed, Ok(true)) {
+            notify(&self.path, b"heartbeat\n");
+        }
+        refreshed
+    }
+
+    /// Ends the lease: removes it if it still names this caller and tells the watcher, which
+    /// then ends without calling `on_lost`. `Ok(false)` when it no longer names this caller.
+    pub fn release(self) -> io::Result<bool> {
+        let released = with_writer_lock(&self.path, || {
+            let Some(current) = LeaseRecord::read(&self.path) else {
+                return Ok(false);
+            };
+            if current.holder != self.holder {
+                return Ok(false);
+            }
+            fs::remove_file(&self.path)?;
+            Ok(true)
+        })?;
+        if released {
+            notify(&self.path, b"released\n");
+        }
+        Ok(released)
+    }
+}
+
+/// The worker's side of a lease: a watch on the caller that runs `on_lost` once if the caller is
+/// lost and nobody claims the lease within the grace. See the module documentation.
+///
+/// Dropping it stops the watch without calling `on_lost`.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+pub struct CallerLease {
+    stop: Option<StopSender>,
+    thread: Option<thread::JoinHandle<LeaseEnd>>,
+}
+
+impl CallerLease {
+    /// Starts watching the lease at `path`.
+    ///
+    /// `holder` is the caller to watch from the start; `None` takes it from the lease file, and
+    /// with no lease file yet the watch waits, unprotected, until a caller claims one (a worker
+    /// started without a caller has nothing to lose). A holder that is already gone starts the
+    /// grace at once.
+    ///
+    /// Errors here are the watch's own: the caller cannot be proven (`Unsupported` on a host that
+    /// cannot describe processes, `PermissionDenied` when the process cannot be read), the host
+    /// cannot watch it and no heartbeat fallback was allowed, or another watcher already listens
+    /// on this lease (`AddrInUse`).
+    pub fn watch(
+        path: &Path,
+        holder: Option<ProcessIdentity>,
+        options: LeaseOptions,
+        on_lost: impl FnOnce(LeaseLoss) + Send + 'static,
+    ) -> io::Result<CallerLease> {
+        options.validate()?;
+        watcher::watch(path, holder, options, Box::new(on_lost))
+    }
+
+    /// Whether the watch has ended.
+    pub fn is_finished(&self) -> bool {
+        self.thread
+            .as_ref()
+            .is_none_or(thread::JoinHandle::is_finished)
+    }
+
+    /// Waits for the watch to end by itself (the lease is lost, released or fails) and says how.
+    pub fn wait(mut self) -> LeaseEnd {
+        self.join()
+    }
+
+    /// Stops the watch and says how it ended: [`LeaseEnd::Stopped`], or the earlier end if it
+    /// had already ended. `on_lost` does not run for a stop.
+    pub fn stop(mut self) -> LeaseEnd {
+        drop(self.stop.take());
+        self.join()
+    }
+
+    /// Lets the watch run for the life of the process: the stop signal is never sent and the
+    /// thread is not joined.
+    pub fn detach(mut self) {
+        if let Some(stop) = self.stop.take() {
+            stop.leak();
+        }
+        drop(self.thread.take());
+    }
+
+    fn join(&mut self) -> LeaseEnd {
+        match self.thread.take().map(thread::JoinHandle::join) {
+            Some(Ok(end)) => end,
+            Some(Err(_)) => LeaseEnd::Failed("the lease watcher panicked".to_string()),
+            None => LeaseEnd::Stopped,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+impl Drop for CallerLease {
+    fn drop(&mut self) {
+        // Closing the stop end wakes the watcher and it leaves; it is not joined, because it may
+        // be inside `on_lost`, which can take as long as the app's cancellation does.
+        drop(self.stop.take());
+    }
+}
+
+#[cfg(unix)]
+mod watcher {
+    use super::{
+        bell_path, CallerLease, LeaseEnd, LeaseLoss, LeaseOptions, LeaseRecord, LossReason,
+    };
+    use crate::{
+        doorbell::{stop_pair, Doorbell, StopReceiver},
+        exit_watch::{poll_readable, ExitWatch},
+        process_identity::{verify, AdoptError, ProcessIdentity},
+    };
+    use std::{
+        cell::Cell,
+        fs, io,
+        os::fd::RawFd,
+        path::{Path, PathBuf},
+        thread,
+        time::{Duration, Instant, SystemTime},
+    };
+
+    /// A heartbeat ttl for a fallback that was allowed without one cannot occur (validated), but
+    /// a value is needed to compute with.
+    const DEFAULT_TTL: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);
+
+    pub(super) fn watch(
+        path: &Path,
+        holder: Option<ProcessIdentity>,
+        options: LeaseOptions,
+        on_lost: Box<dyn FnOnce(LeaseLoss) + Send>,
+    ) -> io::Result<CallerLease> {
+        // `AddrInUse` if another watcher listens here. Before anything is spawned.
+        let bell = Doorbell::create(&bell_path(path)?, false)?;
+        let (sender, receiver) = stop_pair()?;
+        let watcher = Watcher {
+            path: path.to_path_buf(),
+            options,
+            bell,
+            stop: receiver,
+            seen: Cell::new(false),
+        };
+        // The first state is resolved here so the caller sees the watch's own errors.
+        let first = watcher.first_state(holder)?;
+        let thread = thread::Builder::new()
+            .name("rightkit-caller-lease".into())
+            .spawn(move || watcher.run(first, on_lost))?;
+        Ok(CallerLease {
+            stop: Some(sender),
+            thread: Some(thread),
+        })
+    }
+
+    enum State {
+        /// No lease yet: nothing to protect.
+        Unclaimed,
+        /// A caller is believed alive and is watched.
+        Held(Held),
+        /// The caller was lost; a new caller may still claim the lease until `until`.
+        Grace {
+            lost: ProcessIdentity,
+            reason: LossReason,
+            until: Instant,
+        },
+    }
+
+    struct Held {
+        identity: ProcessIdentity,
+        /// The kernel's exit event for the caller; `None` in heartbeat mode.
+        watch: Option<ExitWatch>,
+        /// Heartbeat mode: when the lease expires unless a heartbeat arrives.
+        heartbeat_until: Option<Instant>,
+    }
+
+    enum Engaged {
+        Held(Held),
+        /// The caller named was already gone, or is not the process the lease records.
+        Lost(LossReason),
+    }
+
+    enum Wake {
+        Stop,
+        Bell,
+        Exit,
+        Timeout,
+        Failed(String),
+    }
+
+    struct Watcher {
+        path: PathBuf,
+        options: LeaseOptions,
+        bell: Doorbell,
+        stop: StopReceiver,
+        /// Whether the lease file has been seen to exist. A file that was there and is gone was
+        /// released; a file that was never there (the caller handed its identity over on the
+        /// command line) was not.
+        seen: Cell<bool>,
+    }
+
+    impl Watcher {
+        /// The lease file: `Some(None)` when it was there and is gone (released), `None` when
+        /// there is nothing to learn from it: it never existed, or it exists but cannot be read
+        /// as a lease (keep the state; do not read a hiccup as a release).
+        fn read(&self) -> Option<Option<LeaseRecord>> {
+            match fs::symlink_metadata(&self.path) {
+                Err(error) if error.kind() == io::ErrorKind::NotFound => {
+                    self.seen.get().then_some(None)
+                }
+                Err(_) => None,
+                Ok(_) => {
+                    self.seen.set(true);
+                    LeaseRecord::read(&self.path).map(Some)
+                }
+            }
+        }
+
+        fn first_state(&self, holder: Option<ProcessIdentity>) -> io::Result<State> {
+            let record = self.read().flatten();
+            let identity = match (holder, &record) {
+                (Some(identity), _) => identity,
+                (None, Some(record)) => record.holder.clone(),
+                (None, None) => return Ok(State::Unclaimed),
+            };
+            Ok(match self.engage(identity.clone(), record.as_ref())? {
+                Engaged::Held(held) => State::Held(held),
+                Engaged::Lost(reason) => self.grace(identity, reason),
+            })
+        }
+
+        fn grace(&self, lost: ProcessIdentity, reason: LossReason) -> State {
+            State::Grace {
+                lost,
+                reason,
+                until: Instant::now() + self.options.grace,
+            }
+        }
+
+        fn ttl(&self) -> Duration {
+            self.options.heartbeat_ttl.unwrap_or(DEFAULT_TTL)
+        }
+
+        /// When a lease with this record's heartbeat expires.
+        fn heartbeat_deadline(&self, record: Option<&LeaseRecord>) -> Instant {
+            let age = record
+                .and_then(|record| SystemTime::now().duration_since(record.heartbeat).ok())
+                .unwrap_or(Duration::ZERO);
+            Instant::now() + self.ttl().saturating_sub(age)
+        }
+
+        fn fresh(&self, record: &LeaseRecord) -> bool {
+            match SystemTime::now().duration_since(record.heartbeat) {
+                Ok(age) => age < self.ttl(),
+                Err(_) => true, // stamped in the future: not stale
+            }
+        }
+
+        fn heartbeat_held(&self, identity: ProcessIdentity, record: Option<&LeaseRecord>) -> Held {
+            Held {
+                identity,
+                watch: None,
+                heartbeat_until: Some(self.heartbeat_deadline(record)),
+            }
+        }
+
+        /// Starts watching `identity`. The exit watch is opened first and the identity proven
+        /// after, so the proof names the very process the watch is bound to.
+        fn engage(
+            &self,
+            identity: ProcessIdentity,
+            record: Option<&LeaseRecord>,
+        ) -> io::Result<Engaged> {
+            if self.options.heartbeat_only {
+                return Ok(Engaged::Held(self.heartbeat_held(identity, record)));
+            }
+            let watch = ExitWatch::open(identity.pid, false);
+            match verify(&identity) {
+                Ok(verified) if verified.zombie => {
+                    return Ok(Engaged::Lost(LossReason::HolderExited))
+                }
+                Ok(_) => {}
+                Err(AdoptError::Gone) => return Ok(Engaged::Lost(LossReason::HolderExited)),
+                Err(AdoptError::Replaced { .. } | AdoptError::ExecutableMismatch { .. }) => {
+                    return Ok(Engaged::Lost(LossReason::HolderReplaced))
+                }
+                Err(other) => return Err(other.into()),
+            }
+            if !watch.is_supported() {
+                if self.options.heartbeat_ttl.is_some() {
+                    return Ok(Engaged::Held(self.heartbeat_held(identity, record)));
+                }
+                return Err(io::Error::new(
+                    io::ErrorKind::Unsupported,
+                    "this host cannot watch another process's exit and no heartbeat fallback was allowed",
+                ));
+            }
+            Ok(Engaged::Held(Held {
+                identity,
+                watch: Some(watch),
+                heartbeat_until: None,
+            }))
+        }
+
+        /// A claim that names a live process becomes the watched caller. During the grace a claim
+        /// that names a dead process is ignored (it must not restart the grace).
+        fn adopt(&self, record: LeaseRecord, during_grace: bool) -> Option<State> {
+            let identity = record.holder.clone();
+            match self.engage(identity.clone(), Some(&record)) {
+                Ok(Engaged::Held(held)) => Some(State::Held(held)),
+                Ok(Engaged::Lost(reason)) if !during_grace => Some(self.grace(identity, reason)),
+                _ => None,
+            }
+        }
+
+        /// The lease file changed while a caller is held.
+        fn follow(&self, held: Held, record: LeaseRecord) -> State {
+            if record.holder == held.identity {
+                return self.refresh(held, Some(&record));
+            }
+            match self.engage(record.holder.clone(), Some(&record)) {
+                Ok(Engaged::Held(next)) => State::Held(next),
+                // A claim that does not name a live process changes nothing.
+                _ => State::Held(held),
+            }
+        }
+
+        fn refresh(&self, mut held: Held, record: Option<&LeaseRecord>) -> State {
+            if held.heartbeat_until.is_some() {
+                held.heartbeat_until = Some(self.heartbeat_deadline(record));
+            }
+            State::Held(held)
+        }
+
+        /// Sleeps in `poll(2)` on the stop signal, the doorbell and (if given) the caller's exit
+        /// event, for at most `timeout`.
+        fn wait(&self, exit_fd: Option<RawFd>, timeout: Option<Duration>) -> Wake {
+            let mut fds = vec![self.stop.raw_fd(), self.bell.reader().raw_fd()];
+            fds.extend(exit_fd);
+            match poll_readable(&fds, timeout) {
+                Err(error) => Wake::Failed(error.to_string()),
+                Ok(ready) if ready[0] => Wake::Stop,
+                Ok(ready) if ready.get(2).copied().unwrap_or(false) => Wake::Exit,
+                Ok(ready) if ready[1] => {
+                    let _ = self.bell.reader().drain();
+                    Wake::Bell
+                }
+                Ok(_) => Wake::Timeout,
+            }
+        }
+
+        fn run(&self, first: State, on_lost: impl FnOnce(LeaseLoss)) -> LeaseEnd {
+            let mut state = first;
+            loop {
+                state = match state {
+                    State::Unclaimed => {
+                        match self.wait(None, None) {
+                            Wake::Stop => return LeaseEnd::Stopped,
+                            Wake::Failed(why) => return LeaseEnd::Failed(why),
+                            Wake::Bell | Wake::Exit | Wake::Timeout => {}
+                        }
+                        match self.read() {
+                            Some(Some(record)) => {
+                                self.adopt(record, false).unwrap_or(State::Unclaimed)
+                            }
+                            _ => State::Unclaimed,
+                        }
+                    }
+                    State::Held(held) => {
+                        // An exit that happened before the watch was opened has no descriptor
+                        // to wake on; the watch itself remembers it.
+                        let exited = match &held.watch {
+                            Some(watch) => match watch.wait(Some(Duration::ZERO)) {
+                                Ok(fired) => fired,
+                                Err(error) => return LeaseEnd::Failed(error.to_string()),
+                            },
+                            None => false,
+                        };
+                        if exited {
+                            self.grace(held.identity, LossReason::HolderExited)
+                        } else {
+                            let timeout = held
+                                .heartbeat_until
+                                .map(|at| at.saturating_duration_since(Instant::now()));
+                            let exit_fd = held.watch.as_ref().and_then(ExitWatch::event_fd);
+                            match self.wait(exit_fd, timeout) {
+                                Wake::Stop => return LeaseEnd::Stopped,
+                                Wake::Failed(why) => return LeaseEnd::Failed(why),
+                                Wake::Exit => self.grace(held.identity, LossReason::HolderExited),
+                                Wake::Bell => match self.read() {
+                                    Some(None) => return LeaseEnd::Released,
+                                    Some(Some(record)) => self.follow(held, record),
+                                    None => State::Held(held),
+                                },
+                                Wake::Timeout if held.heartbeat_until.is_none() => {
+                                    State::Held(held)
+                                }
+                                Wake::Timeout => match self.read() {
+                                    Some(None) => return LeaseEnd::Released,
+                                    Some(Some(record)) if record.holder != held.identity => {
+                                        self.follow(held, record)
+                                    }
+                                    Some(Some(record)) if self.fresh(&record) => {
+                                        self.refresh(held, Some(&record))
+                                    }
+                                    _ => self.grace(held.identity, LossReason::HeartbeatExpired),
+                                },
+                            }
+                        }
+                    }
+                    State::Grace {
+                        lost,
+                        reason,
+                        until,
+                    } => {
+                        let remaining = until.saturating_duration_since(Instant::now());
+                        if !remaining.is_zero() {
+                            match self.wait(None, Some(remaining)) {
+                                Wake::Stop => return LeaseEnd::Stopped,
+                                Wake::Failed(why) => return LeaseEnd::Failed(why),
+                                Wake::Bell | Wake::Exit | Wake::Timeout => {}
+                            }
+                        }
+                        // Read at every wake-up and once more when the grace is over: a claim
+                        // whose ring was lost still counts if it was written in time.
+                        let claim = match self.read() {
+                            Some(None) => return LeaseEnd::Released,
+                            Some(Some(record))
+                                if record.holder != lost
+                                    || (self.options.heartbeat_only && self.fresh(&record)) =>
+                            {
+                                self.adopt(record, true)
+                            }
+                            _ => None,
+                        };
+                        match claim {
+                            Some(next) => next,
+                            None if Instant::now() >= until => {
+                                let loss = LeaseLoss {
+                                    holder: lost,
+                                    reason,
+                                    grace: self.options.grace,
+                                };
+                                on_lost(loss.clone());
+                                return LeaseEnd::Lost(loss);
+                            }
+                            None => State::Grace {
+                                lost,
+                                reason,
+                                until,
+                            },
+                        }
+                    }
+                };
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+#[cfg(windows)]
+mod watcher {
+    use super::{CallerLease, LeaseLoss, LeaseOptions};
+    use crate::process_identity::ProcessIdentity;
+    use std::{io, path::Path};
+
+    /// WINDOWS SESSION: implement per the module documentation of `caller_lease`. Until then a
+    /// lease cannot be watched on this host; [`LeaseClaim`](super::LeaseClaim) still writes the
+    /// lease file.
+    pub(super) fn watch(
+        _path: &Path,
+        _holder: Option<ProcessIdentity>,
+        _options: LeaseOptions,
+        _on_lost: Box<dyn FnOnce(LeaseLoss) + Send>,
+    ) -> io::Result<CallerLease> {
+        Err(io::Error::from(io::ErrorKind::Unsupported))
+    }
+}
